Description: Put applications into their systemd scope before they start.
 GNOME Shell, gnome-session and gnome-settings-daemon ask systemd to move each application they launch with
 GDesktopAppInfo into a scope of its own, app-gnome-<application ID>-<pid>.scope, but only once GLib has started it.
 systemd moves only the process it is given, so every process the application started in the meantime stays in the
 cgroup of the service which launched it. Chromium and Electron applications start their zygotes and their GPU process
 at once, which then stay in GNOME Shell's or gnome-session's cgroup, are stopped with it before the application at
 logout, and the application crashes when they go.
 .
 gio-launch-desktop, which GLib runs the application through, asks systemd for the scope instead, for itself, and
 waits until systemd has moved it into the scope before it executes the application, which therefore starts every
 process of its own in the scope. gio-launch-desktop stays connected to the session bus until systemd has answered,
 since systemd checks the caller on the bus before it acts on a request, and refuses the request of a caller which is
 gone by then, as a launching process which exits right after launching, like gio open behind xdg-open, often is.
 When systemd refused the scope, or the job starting it ended with another result than done, gio-launch-desktop runs
 the application at once. Otherwise it waits as long as systemd needs, so that a busy systemd delays the application
 instead of splitting it, and gives up after 60 seconds. GLib only names the scope, in the environment of
 gio-launch-desktop, which links against GIO on Linux for its request.
 .
 The scope is requested only for processes managed by the systemd user instance, outside of sandboxes, and only when
 systemd is on the session bus, which the bus answers itself, and the name is short enough for systemd, as those
 refusals are certain. The process is given to systemd as a pidfd, when the bus can pass one, as systemd would look up
 a process ID in its own PID namespace, which is not the one of a process in a sandbox of its own which uses the
 session bus. The process counts as in the scope also in a cgroup below the scope's.
 .
 gio-launch-desktop logs at error priority when the application runs outside of its scope after all, with the reason
 systemd gave, or after the 60 seconds. Neither is expected. A refusal because systemd is stopping, at the end of a
 session, is logged at notice priority. When standard error is the journal, whose stream gives every line the same
 priority, the message is sent through syslog, so that it keeps its priority.
 .
 The scope has the name gnome-desktop's gnome_start_systemd_scope() gives it, so that when a launching process asks
 for it too, systemd creates it for whichever request it receives first, with the same process, and the other fails
 without moving any process, and so that gnome-session's drop-in for app-gnome-*.scope applies. The "launched" signal
 names the scope as "systemd-unit" in its platform data, so that launching processes can leave the scope to GLib.
Author: Mitar
Bug: https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/merge_requests/1596
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-09

